{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791210084,"nonce":"xn/3iL7m4Q2aU6d+aQvm1eqYkCY=","title":"rayzor run never promotes hot code past Cranelift baseline","message":"With the default Application preset, a program whose hot functions are called from compiled code stays entirely on Baseline (Cranelift). Measured on the nuc at c8147a87 with a generated Main.hx: 200 static functions, each called 60000 times from a loop in main:\n\n    rayzor run --no-cache --release --stats Main.hx\n    Tiered Compilation: 0 Interpreted (P0), 203 Baseline (P1), 0 Standard (P2), 0 Optimized (P3), 0 LLVM (P4)\n    Profile: 1 functions (0 hot, 0 warm, 1 cold), 1 total calls\n    Beadie: ... installs=0 ... compiled=0\n\nRAYZOR_PROFILE_TIER_EVENTS=1 shows no llvm_compile_scope; RAYZOR_OSR_TRACE=1 shows no transfer. nbody through the CLI: 23 functions, all Baseline.\n\nWhy: native direct calls do not pass through TieredBackend::record_call (compile_llvm_bead says so), so only main is counted, and it is entered once. OSR resume points are created by compile_llvm_bead itself, i.e. only after a promotion was requested, so a long-running main loop never gets one. The benchmark runner hides this: it calls main repeatedly through execute_function, so main crosses the threshold.\n\nImpact: every long-running CLI program runs Cranelift code. For kernels where LLVM matters (mandelbrot: cranelift 257ms vs llvm 221ms on the nuc; fibonacci 256 vs 270 is the other way) the tiering preset described as \"balanced tiering with LLVM (default)\" never delivers LLVM.\n\nBefore making promotion fire more often, see the promotion-memory issue: each promotion clones every loaded module and leaks its LLVM context and IR.","files":null}]}